<h1><span lang="EN-GB">Alignment Procedure</span></h1>
<div class="Normal" style="text-align:center;" align="center">
<hr align="center" size="5" class="whs3"></div>
<p class="description"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span> Alignment Procedure specifies how to do alignment.</p>
<p class="stepstitle">Procedure</p>
<ul type="disc" class="whs4">
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet">Ensure the localiser hardware and software setup is complete</p>
</li>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet">Run KUBE</p>
</li>
</ul>
<table class="NormalTable" style="margin-left:19px; left:0px; top:195px; width:473px; height:156px; border-spacing:0px;" cellspacing="0" width="473">
<col class="whs5">
<col class="whs6">
<tr class="whs7">
<td height="65" valign="top" width="307px" class="whs8">
<p class="normal-table">&#160;</p>
<p class="normal-table">The power light on the sensor illuminates. Leave the sensor switched on for <b style="font-weight:normal;"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:normal;">30</span> minutes</b> before proceeding.</p>
<p class="Note"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Note:</b></span> This is not applied to MMD and newer sensors.</p>
</td>
<td height="65" width="161px" class="whs9">
<p class="normal-table"><img id="_x0000_i1026" src="power_clock.jpg" width="150px" height="150px" border="0" class="img_whs10" alt=""></p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet">Select the alignment icon <span lang="EN-GB"><img id="_x0000_i1027" src="alignment_buton.jpg" width="37px" height="28px" border="0" class="img_whs11" alt=""> or go to</span> <i style="font-weight:bold;"><span class="Path">System &gt; Alignment</span></i> <span lang="EN-GB">or press</span> <span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Ctrl + Shift + Q</b></span><span lang="EN-GB">.</span></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="Normal">&#160;</p>
<p class="stepstitle">Alignment Wizard - Step One</p>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet">Enter the correct certified distances of the alignment block into the alignment dialogue:</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="Normal"><img src="Alignment_files/alignment.gif" width="552px" height="659px" border="0" class="img_whs12" alt=""></p>
<p class="sub-head-steptitle"><b style="font-weight:bold;">Use Certified Settings</b></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><b><span style="font-weight:normal;">It is recommended to check this box to use predefined alignment settings for each combination of sensor and localiser. If any of the settings need to be changed, please refer to the description of &#160;<a href="alignment.htm">Alignment Command</a>.</span></b></p>
<p class="sub-head-steptitle"><b style="font-weight:bold;">Localiser movement tolerance</b></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">The <b style="font-weight:normal;">localiser movement tolerance</b> allows for a certain amount of movement during data capture. <a href="alignment.htm#tolerance"><img src="../Images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs13" alt=""></a></p>
<p class="sub-head-steptitle">Certified Distances</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">For certified system performance the 'C<b style="font-weight:normal;">ertified distances'</b> must be used. Enter the distances marked on your alignment block into the relevant dialogue boxes. <span lang="EN-US">&#160;</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">&#160;</p>
<p class="stepstitle"><span lang="EN-GB">Alignment Wizard - Step Two</span></p>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet">For each plane, a <b><span style="font-weight:normal;">number of probe points</span> a</b>re captured then an <b style="font-weight:normal;">equal number of individual stripes</b> from the sensor.</p>
</li>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet">The alignment block has numbered planes and data <b style="font-weight:normal;">from each plane</b> should be captured in numerical order, starting with plane 1.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><img src="alignment1.gif" width="552px" height="659px" border="0" class="img_whs12" alt=""></p>
<p class="sub-head-steptitle"><span lang="EN-GB">Capturing probe points (FARO / CimCore)</span></p>
<table class="NormalTable" style="left:0px; top:2312px; margin-left:38px; border-spacing:0px;" cellspacing="0">
<col class="whs14">
<tr class="whs15">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="2" valign="top" width="701px" class="whs16">
<p class="normal-table">The ball probe should be positioned near normal to the plane. Points are captured. A 'beep' will sound when the point is captured. If unsure whether the point was captured correctly, then delete it and capture another.</p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr class="whs15">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="2" valign="top" width="701px" class="whs16">
<p class="Normal" style="margin-right:0px; text-align:center;" align="center"><img id="_x0000_i1029" src="alignment_block_number.gif" width="150px" height="140px" border="0" class="img_whs17" alt=""></p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p class="sub-head-steptitle"><span lang="EN-GB">Capturing probe points (K-Scan)</span></p>
<table class="NormalTable" style="left:0px; top:2312px; margin-left:38px; border-spacing:0px;" cellspacing="0">
<col class="whs18">
<tr class="whs15">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="2" valign="top" width="703px" class="whs19">
<p class="normal-table">Probe points are captured using the SpaceProbe. In order to minimise the error make sure that the probe tip is oriented in the same way for each point from a plane. Press ‘B’ button when SpaceProbe is stable. A 'beep' will sound when the point is captured. If unsure whether the point was captured correctly, delete it and capture another. &#160;</p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr class="whs15">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="2" valign="top" width="703px" class="whs19">
<p class="Normal" style="margin-right:0px;"><img src="Alignment_files/probe_capture.gif" width="702px" height="808px" border="0" class="img_whs20" alt=""></p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p class="parameterdescription">Make sure that the probe point positions are distributed over the plane.</p>
<table class="NormalTable" style="margin-left:38px; width:95.608%; border-spacing:0px;" cellspacing="0" width="95.608%">
<col>
<col class="whs21">
<tr class="whs22">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="205" valign="top" class="whs23">
<p class="Normal"><img src="alignment2.gif" width="552px" height="659px" border="0" class="img_whs12" alt=""></p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="205" width="294px" class="whs24">
<p class="normal-table">As the data capture proceeds, the plane fit RMS is displayed. Recapture any incorrect points as required.</p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr class="whs22">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="205" valign="top" class="whs23">
<p class="Normal"><img src="alignment3.gif" width="552px" height="659px" border="0" class="img_whs12" alt=""></p>
</td>
<td height="205" width="294px" class="whs24">
<p class="normal-table">The estimated RMS is updated at each captured probe point. If the value is too high, the result will be displayed in red (as shown on left) and a warning sound will be played.</p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p class="Note" id="P1"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Note:</b></span> The RMS will depend on the localiser being used, as a rough guide all RMS figures should be well below 0.1mm. <span style="margin-left:40px;">Please refer to manufacturers specifications for expected values.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="margin-left:40px;">&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head-steptitle"><span lang="EN-GB">Capturing stripes</span></p>
<table cellspacing="0" class="whs25">
<col>
<col>
<tr valign="top" class="whs26">
<td class="whs27">
<p class="normal-table"><img id="_x0000_i1031" src="alignment_block_stripes.gif" width="150px" height="124px" border="0" class="img_whs28" alt=""></p>
</td>
<td valign="middle" class="whs29">
<p class="normal-table">Point the stripe at the plane and check the stripe display on the monitor.</p>
<p class="normal-table">&#160;</p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p class="parameterdescription">This will produce a screen view like one of those shown below:</p>
<table cellspacing="0" class="whs25">
<col>
<col>
<tr valign="top" class="whs26">
<td class="whs27">
<p class="normal-table"><img id="_x0000_i1032" src="alignment_wizard_stripes.gif" width="250px" height="152px" border="0" class="img_whs30" alt=""></p>
</td>
<td valign="middle" class="whs29">
<p class="normal-table"><b style="font-weight:bold;">Above:</b> Valid data (correct angle and standoff)</p>
<p class="normal-table"><b style="font-weight:bold;">Below:</b> Invalid data (incorrect angle and standoff)</p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p class="parameterdescription">A 'beep' will sound when the stripe is correctly captured. For a stripe to be captured, the stripe as shown on the display, must be green in colour. This happens when the sensor is at the correct standoff and the correct roll angle.</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">The sensor must be held steady. The system will accept a stripe when the sensor has moved less than movement tolerance between two measurements. If holding the sensor steady is difficult, try increasing the stripe capture tolerance.</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">To produce an accurate alignment the stripes are captured from 15 to 60 degrees to the normal of the surface of the plane.</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">It is important to distribute the stripes over a range of localiser angles. Below is a typical pattern for 9 stripes.</p>
<table class="NormalTable" style="margin-left:36.0pt; border-spacing:0px;" cellspacing="0">
<col>
<col>
<tr class="whs31">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="94" valign="top" class="whs32">
<p class="Normal" style="margin-left:0px;"><img id="_x0000_i1033" src="align_sensor_stripes1.gif" width="149px" height="127px" border="0" class="img_whs33" alt=""></p>
</td>
<td height="94" class="whs34">
<p class="Normal" style="margin-left:0px;">For the first three stripes the sensor is held <b style="font-weight:normal;">vertically</b>, 15 to 60 degrees to the left.</p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr class="whs31">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="94" valign="top" class="whs32">
<p class="Normal" style="margin-left:0px;"><img id="_x0000_i1034" src="align_sensor_stripes2.gif" width="150px" height="136px" border="0" class="img_whs35" alt=""></p>
</td>
<td height="94" class="whs34">
<p class="Normal" style="margin-left:0px;">For the second three stripes the sensor is held <b style="font-weight:normal;">vertically</b>, 15 to 60 degrees to the right.</p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr class="whs31">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="94" valign="top" class="whs32">
<p class="Normal" style="margin-left:0px;"><img id="_x0000_i1035" src="align_sensor_stripes3.gif" width="150px" height="132px" border="0" class="img_whs36" alt=""></p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" height="94" class="whs34">
<p class="Normal" style="margin-left:0px;">For the last three stripes the sensor is held <b style="font-weight:normal;">horizontally</b>, 15 to 60 degrees upwards.</p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr class="whs31">
<td colspan="2" rowspan="1" height="94" valign="top" class="whs32">
<p>Stripe positions on a plane and K-Scan orientation</p>
<p><img src="Alignment_files/stripe_capture1.gif" width="644px" height="239px" border="0" class="img_whs37" alt=""></p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p class="parameterdescription">At the end of the last stripe on this plane, the RMS is calculated. If the RMS is unsatisfactory, recapture the plane.</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">When the plane is complete, repeat the data capture process for the remaining planes in sequence i.e. 2, 3, 4 and 5.</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">At the end of the last stripe on the last plane, the alignment will automatically be generated.</p>
<p class="Normal">&#160;</p>
<p class="stepstitle"><span lang="EN-GB">Alignment Wizard - Step Three</span></p>
<p>The alignment results will now be displayed.</p>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet">Check that the probe RMS figures match the expected performance of the localiser. In particular, check that the plane errors for the probe match the expected performance of the localiser. Large probe errors indicate poor probe calibration or poor localiser performance.</p>
</li>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet">Check that the stripe RMS figures match the expected performance of the localiser.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Although the results screen can indicate whether the alignment has been successful, it is necessary to perform a <a href="Alignment_Check.htm" style="font-family:Arial, sans-serif; font-size:10pt;">system certification check</a> to certify performance.</p>
<p class="Normal">&#160;</p>
<p class="stepstitle"><span lang="EN-GB">Loading and saving sensor alignment data</span></p>
<p>The alignment information is stored on your PC immediately after an alignment has taken place. The file name reflects the localiser you are using and the sensor serial number.</p>
<p>When you change sensors or localisers, KUBE will automatically try and load the correct alignment file, based on the serial number of your sensor.</p>
<p>This capability is useful where one sensor is being used on two or more different localisers, each with their own arm mount. The sensor can be repeatably fastened to the localiser mount. The localiser mount must not be removed or adjusted.</p>
<p class="Warning" id="P4"><font id="font4">If any of the localiser mounts are removed or adjusted, then the alignment will be lost and a new alignment must be carried out.</font></p>
